From b042b7b7dacd55a439e91e6e6c6691c0f4745b6f Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: reaper <theunbounddeveloper@outlook.com>
Date: Tue, 8 Sep 2026 23:12:17 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] www/dnsmasq: the ECS help text stopped claiming AdGuard Home
 filters per client

Owner, 2026-09-07: "Fix wording of ECS in the failover function GUI. Currently
it reads '...still shows and filters per client.' which is only half true."

RDHC_22 described "Pass client addresses to the DNS server (EDNS Client
Subnet)" as making AdGuard Home or Pi-hole "still show and filter per client".
That is true of Pi-hole and false of AdGuard Home, which is the filter this
firmware's own Failover page steers people toward.

AdGuard Home identifies a client by ClientID, then source IP, then CIDR, then
MAC. The ECS value is recorded in the query log and is never an input to rule
selection - asked for upstream twice and declined (AdGuardHome #4383, #6104).
So with dnsmasq forwarding, every per-client rule on an AdGuard target is
inert, and no setting of this switch changes that: ECS cannot recover an
identity the proxy has already erased. A user reading the old text would turn
the option on, see client addresses appear in the query log, and conclude the
per-client rules they had written were working.

The replacement says what each filter actually does with the value, and stops
short of promising behaviour on the one the text was most likely to be read
against. Value-only splice across all 25 dicts, line 6889 in every pack, line
counts unchanged (6907) so the build's line-indexed lookup is undisturbed. The
same correction is carried into the start_dnsmasq() comment, so the next reader
of the code is not re-taught the wrong thing.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>
