diff --git a/release/src/router/www/EN.dict b/release/src/router/www/EN.dict
index f88278bbef..a240f220ef 100644
--- a/release/src/router/www/EN.dict
+++ b/release/src/router/www/EN.dict
@@ -6886,7 +6886,7 @@ RDHC_18=Upstream order
 RDHC_19=Strict order (dnsmasq strict-order)
 RDHC_20=Client addresses
 RDHC_21=Pass client addresses to the DNS server (EDNS Client Subnet)
-RDHC_22=With every client sent through the router, the DNS server would see only the router. This attaches the asking client's address to each forwarded query, so AdGuard Home (with its "Use EDNS Client Subnet" setting on) or Pi-hole (on by default) still shows and filters per client.
+RDHC_22=With every client sent through the router, the DNS server sees only the router. This attaches the address of the asking client to each forwarded query. Pi-hole reads that address and keeps showing and filtering per client. AdGuard Home shows it in the query log but does not use it to pick which rules apply, so its per-client rules stay inactive while the router is forwarding.
 RDHC_23=Router DNS cache
 RDHC_24=Do not cache DNS answers on the router
 RDHC_25=The router normally keeps answers for a while. With per-client filtering upstream, a cached answer decided for one client would be handed to another, and the filter would not see the cached lookups at all. Off, the router only forwards and the DNS server keeps the cache.
